  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B043",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B013",
    "B020"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B013 to B043. Optical lattice clocks have the attributes that a final choice should demand simultaneously: a sharply stated physical invention, decisive realization, exceptional and independently reproducible performance, and a durable new capability for precision measurement and fundamental tests. The magic-wavelength lattice supplies the essential mechanism, namely interrogation of many atoms without the usual trapping perturbation. Katori and Ye provide a compact division of credit between the originating concept and defining experimental development. This is not merely technical optimization, but a controlled atomic regime with mature consequences across precision physics.",
    "case_against": "The citation must remain confined to the original lattice-clock invention and realization, rather than absorbing frequency-comb work, later clock architectures, comparison methods, or all subsequent metrological applications. B043 is an enabling measurement technology rather than a discovery of a new natural phenomenon; its selection therefore rests on the unusual conceptual clarity, demonstrated maturity, and well-delimited attribution of the underlying advance.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the magic-wavelength lattice concept, decisive realization, reproducibility, and Katori-Ye attribution make B043 an unusually complete and cleanly bounded achievement.",
        "response": "I agree, especially with the emphasis on the physical mechanism rather than on headline accuracy alone. His distinction between the original advance and the later metrological ecosystem supplies the boundary that I found necessary before moving from KPZ to B043. A prize for invention and realization can recognize the new controlled atomic measurement regime without claiming credit for every subsequent application."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth regards B043 as a mature atomic-control advance with a coherent two-person attribution, while cautioning that it should not be credited with resolving the fundamental questions it enables.",
        "response": "I share that restraint. The case is strongest when framed as an advance in quantum control and measurement, not as an award for downstream tests. This precise framing also makes B043 preferable to my earlier B013 configuration: KPZ has extraordinary conceptual unity, but Zhang's unavoidable absence leaves the foundational theoretical credit materially incomplete."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that B020 is mature and broadly predictive, but recognizes that its distributed development requires a careful delimitation of foundational credit.",
        "response": "DMFT remains my strongest theoretical alternative because it made a major class of correlated-electron problems quantitatively tractable. Yet the same breadth that makes it powerful complicates a prize boundary. B043 has a more discrete central mechanism, a directly identifiable realization, and a more secure two-person credit line."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical record confirms that Katori and Ye alone delimit the indispensable invention and defining realization, rather than requiring credit for additional enabling contributors.",
      "How narrowly the citation must be interpreted to exclude later clock architectures and metrological refinements."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "I share the closely linked Gothenburg physics environment with Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson; the supplied profile identifies no specific collaboration with either, and neither is a proposed laureate. No recusal is indicated."
  }
}
